{"id":"a93525ca-1b2b-4d34-bfbf-20fe6fea5e7f","object":"chat.completion","created":1785018384,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    word_token_indices = case[\\\"word_token_indices\\\"]\\n    word_labels = case[\\\"word_labels\\\"]\\n    token_count = case[\\\"token_count\\\"]\\n\\n    if len(word_token_indices) != len(word_labels):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Mismatched word_token_indices and word_labels lengths\\\")\\n\\n    all_indices = set()\\n    for idx_list in word_token_indices:\\n        for idx in idx_list:\\n            if not (0 <= idx < token_count):\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Index {idx} out of range 0..{token_count-1}\\\")\\n            if idx in all_indices:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Duplicate index {idx}\\\")\\n            all_indices.add(idx)\\n\\n    if all_indices != set(range(token_count)):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Token indices do not cover all tokens exactly\\\")\\n\\n    token_labels = [-1] * token_count\\n    for indices, label in zip(word_token_indices, word_labels):\\n        for idx in indices:\\n            token_labels[idx] = label\\n\\n    kept_indices = [i for i, label in enumerate(token_labels) if label == 1]\\n\\n    return {\\n        \\\"kept_indices\\\": kept_indices,\\n        \\\"token_labels\\\": token_labels\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We are given a task: NLP-LL2-01. We need to implement `solve(case)` that takes a payload and returns a JSON object with `kept_indices` and `token_labels`. The payload contains `word_token_indices` (list of lists of ints), `word_labels` (list of ints), `token_count` (int), and possibly `_case_nonce`. The objective is to project word-level keep/drop labels onto subword spans. Each word has a list of token indices. For each word group, broadcast its label to all token indices in that group. Then, produce a `token_labels` array of length `token_count`, where each position gets the label from the word that contains it. Then, `kept_indices` are those indices where the label equals 1. Must reject overlapping, out-of-range, or uncovered token indices. So we need to validate that the union of all token indices covers exactly all indices 0 to token_count-1, no overlaps, no gaps, no out-of-range indices.\n\nThe output schema: `kept_indices` is array of integers, `token_labels` array of integers (0 or 1). Must return both.\n\nImplementation: Use Python standard library only.\n\nEdge cases: token_count may be larger than covered? Possibly uncovered tokens? According to requirement \"Reject overlapping, out-of-range, or uncovered token indices.\" So if there are uncovered tokens (indices not in any word group), we should raise an error? But the function must return a JSON-serializable object. The instruction says \"Validate every required field before computing a result.\" If validation fails, maybe we should return something? But the spec doesn't say what to do on invalid input. Typically in these tasks, the input is valid. But we should still check and if invalid, we could raise an exception, but that would cause the evaluation to fail? It's safer to assume inputs are valid. However, the instructions say \"Reject overlapping, out-of-range, or uncovered token indices.\" That suggests we should detect and handle. But the output contract doesn't include an error field. Possibly we should raise ValueError? But the function must return a JSON object. I think we should implement validation and if fails, raise an exception. The evaluator likely provides valid inputs. We'll implement validation and raise ValueError with message.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Extract fields: word_token_indices, word_labels, token_count.\n2. Validate:\n   - word_token_indices and word_labels are lists of same length.\n   - Each word_token_indices is a list of ints.\n   - All token indices are valid: 0 <= idx < token_count.\n   - No overlap: set of all indices equals distinct set with no duplicates. Use a set: all_indices = set(); for indices in word_token_indices: if any idx in all_indices: overlap error; else add.\n   - After processing all words, check that the set of all indices equals set(range(token_count)). If not, uncovered or out-of-order.\n3. Initialize token_labels array of length token_count with some placeholder, say -1.\n4. For each word, get its label (int), and for each idx in its indices, set token_labels[idx] = label.\n5. After all, verify that no placeholder remains (i.e., all indices assigned). But if we already verified coverage, it's ok.\n6. Build kept_indices = [i for i, label in enumerate(token_labels) if label == 1].\n7. Return {\"kept_indices\": kept_indices, \"token_labels\": token_labels}.\n\nNote: word_labels are integers (0 or 1). The broadcast label: each token gets the word label.\n\nReturn exactly that dictionary.\n\nNow produce the final answer: one JSON object with key \"implementation\" containing Python source as a string (JSON-escaped). Must be a valid JSON object.\n\nWe'll write the code as a function solve(case) that returns the dict.\n\nBe careful to escape backslashes and quotes for JSON.\n\nLet's produce the Python code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    word_token_indices = case[\"word_token_indices\"]\n    word_labels = case[\"word_labels\"]\n    token_count = case[\"token_count\"]\n    \n    # validation\n    if len(word_token_indices) != len(word_labels):\n        raise ValueError(\"Mismatched word_token_indices and word_labels lengths\")\n    \n    all_indices = set()\n    for idx_list in word_token_indices:\n        for idx in idx_list:\n            if not (0 <= idx < token_count):\n                raise ValueError(f\"Index {idx} out of range 0..{token_count-1}\")\n            if idx in all_indices:\n                raise ValueError(f\"Duplicate index {idx}\")\n            all_indices.add(idx)\n    \n    if all_indices != set(range(token_count)):\n        raise ValueError(\"Token indices do not cover all tokens exactly\")\n    \n    token_labels = [-1] * token_count\n    for indices, label in zip(word_token_indices, word_labels):\n        for idx in indices:\n            token_labels[idx] = label\n    \n    kept_indices = [i for i, label in enumerate(token_labels) if label == 1]\n    \n    return {\n        \"kept_indices\": kept_indices,\n        \"token_labels\": token_labels\n    }\n```\n\nNow we need to embed this as a JSON string under \"implementation\". The response must be exactly a JSON object with one key \"implementation\".\n\nLet's create it.\n\nWe also note that the input `case` might have additional fields like `_case_nonce` but we ignore them.\n\nSo final output.\n\nMake sure to escape newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\". Use raw string? No, just regular.\n\nWe'll write the code as a multiline string, then convert to JSON string.\n\nI'll produce the following:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    word_token_indices = case[\\\"word_token_indices\\\"]\\n    word_labels = case[\\\"word_labels\\\"]\\n    token_count = case[\\\"token_count\\\"]\\n    \\n    # validation\\n    if len(word_token_indices) != len(word_labels):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Mismatched word_token_indices and word_labels lengths\\\")\\n    \\n    all_indices = set()\\n    for idx_list in word_token_indices:\\n        for idx in idx_list:\\n            if not (0 <= idx < token_count):\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Index {idx} out of range 0..{token_count-1}\\\")\\n            if idx in all_indices:\\n                raise ValueError(f\\\"Duplicate index {idx}\\\")\\n            all_indices.add(idx)\\n    \\n    if all_indices != set(range(token_count)):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Token indices do not cover all tokens exactly\\\")\\n    \\n    token_labels = [-1] * token_count\\n    for indices, label in zip(word_token_indices, word_labels):\\n        for idx in indices:\\n            token_labels[idx] = label\\n    \\n    kept_indices = [i for i, label in enumerate(token_labels) if label == 1]\\n    \\n    return {\\n        \\\"kept_indices\\\": kept_indices,\\n        \\\"token_labels\\\": token_labels\\n    }\"\n}\n\nDouble-check: The string includes double quotes inside, so we need to escape them with backslash. In the above, I already used escaped quotes in the f-string? Actually in the code I used f\"Index {idx} out of range...\" which is a double-quoted string. That needs to be escaped as \\\"Index {idx} out of range...\\\". Also the dictionary keys and values inside the code use double quotes: \"word_token_indices\", etc. So we must escape those.\n\nLet's rewrite the code with all double quotes escaped. Also the newline characters are represented as \\n.\n\nI'll write it properly.\n\nFinal output should be a single JSON object without any extra text."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1143,"completion_tokens":2022,"total_tokens":3165,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1024},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1707},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1024,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":119},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}